  "careers": "• Architect and Civil Engineer: use scale drawings and models to plan buildings and roads in Nairobi and Mombasa.\n• Surveyor and Cartographer: use scale factors to produce maps, such as the Survey of Kenya maps.\n• Graphic Designer and Photographer: resize images for passports, posters and billboards without distortion.\n• Tailor and Fashion Designer: scale patterns for different sizes of kitenge and school uniform garments.\n• Sign Writer: enlarges logos and painted shop signs in market centres.",
  "focus": "This unit uses the passport photograph puzzle as an anchor for investigating why figures can change size but keep the same shape. Learners measure, predict, construct and explain how corresponding sides and angles, scale factors and the centre of enlargement keep shapes undistorted. They extend this to areas and volumes and apply it in Kenyan contexts such as maps, scale models and posters. Through the lessons they revise a cumulative model and a Driving Question Board.",
  "phenomenon": "A Kenyan student has her full-size photograph taken at a studio in Kisumu for her national ID and passport application. The photographer prints a passport photo of only 3.5 cm × 4.5 cm. Her whole face and shoulders fit into this tiny box, yet she still looks exactly like herself: her face is not stretched, squashed or skewed. The same picture can also be enlarged to a large poster for her school's prize-giving day, and it still looks like her. Yet if the photographer forces the picture into the box by changing only its width, the face looks stretched and distorted. This is puzzling because the size changes by a huge amount, yet the shape is retained. What must stay the same, and what is allowed to change?",
  "supportingPhenomena": "• A map of Kenya in an atlas and a large wall map of Kenya show Lake Victoria and Mombasa with the same shape, though the sizes differ greatly.\n• A matchbox-size model of a matatu looks just like the real matatu, but its paint and material needs are very different.\n• A tailor in Gikomba market cuts small and large versions of the same dress pattern, and the large version needs much more cloth than just twice as much when the length is doubled.\n• A projector in a classroom enlarges a small slide into a large image on the wall, keeping the same shape but flipped when the lens is reversed.",
  "drivingQuestion": "DRIVING QUESTION: How can a full-size photograph of a person be resized to fit a small passport photo box without distortion?\n\nKICD KEY INQUIRY QUESTIONS:\n1. Why do we need to enlarge or reduce objects?\n2. How do we use similarity and enlargement in day-to-day activities?",
  "storylineThread": "Lesson 1: Anchoring phenomenon. Learners predict how a full-size photo fits a 3.5 cm × 4.5 cm box, examine undistorted and distorted passport photos, and build the first Driving Question Board.\nLesson 2: Identifying similar figures in the environment (photos, maps, models, school logos) and sorting similar from non-similar figures.\nLesson 3: Properties of similar figures: measuring corresponding angles and ratios of corresponding sides on original and resized photos.\nLesson 4: Linear scale factor: calculating scale factors for enlargements and reductions, such as a full-size photo to a passport photo, and finding missing lengths.\nLesson 5: Solving problems involving similar triangles and other figures, such as heights of trees or school buildings using shadows.\nLesson 6: Area scale factor: investigating how the area changes when lengths are enlarged by k, using grid paper and cut-outs, and relating it to k².\nLesson 7: Volume scale factor: comparing small and large similar solids such as cartons or containers, and relating volume to k³.\nLesson 8: Introduction to enlargement: centre of enlargement and scale factor, and constructing images with positive scale factors using rays from the centre.\nLesson 9: Enlargement with fractional and negative scale factors, so learners see reduction and inverted images, as in a projector.\nLesson 10: Finding the centre and scale factor of an enlargement from an object and its image by joining corresponding points.\nLesson 11: Applications: scale drawings, maps of Kenya, scale models and costing painted signs, using area and volume factors.\nLesson 12: Consolidation: learners complete the cumulative model, return to the passport photo phenomenon, explain it using ratios, angles and scale factors, and resolve the Driving Question Board."
