    "aresKeywords": "linear scale factor, k, object, image, enlargement, reduction, corresponding sides, missing length, similar (~), original, passport photo, poster",
    "slo": {
      "purpose": "Learners calculate the linear scale factor k = image length ÷ object length for Wanjiru's prints, classify it as an enlargement (k > 1) or reduction (0 < k < 1), and use k to find missing lengths. They also see that the distorted 4.5 × 4.5 cm print has two different factors (9/28 and 1/4), so no single k exists for it.",
      "knowledge": "Learners know that the linear scale factor is k = image length ÷ object length, and they always state which print is the object and which is the image. For original (14 × 18) to passport (3.5 × 4.5), k = 1/4 (a reduction). For original to poster (70 × 90), k = 5 (an enlargement). For passport to poster, k = 20. The distorted print has a width factor of 9/28 (about 0.32) and a height factor of 1/4 (0.25), which are not equal, so it is not similar to the original. Learners know that a missing length = k × object length, or image length ÷ k.",
      "skills": "Learners calculate k from corresponding sides and check that the same k works for width and height. They use k to find missing lengths in either direction, for example the face-triangle eye gap of 5.6 cm becoming 1.4 cm in the passport photo. They justify claims with calculations (NGSS SEP: Using Mathematics and Computational Thinking; Constructing Explanations). They also carry out the NGSS SEP of Analysing and Interpreting Data by comparing the two factors for the distorted print.",
      "attitudes": "Learners show curiosity and precision when calculating and checking factors. They show teamwork and unity in groups, and responsibility by caring for rulers and sharing materials. They value the use of scale factors in day-to-day activities such as photo printing in Kisumu.",
      "keyInquiry": "1. Why do we need to enlarge or reduce objects? 2. How do we use similarity and enlargement in day-to-day activities?",
      "purposeInStoryline": "Lesson 3 showed that similar figures have equal corresponding angles and equal ratios of corresponding sides. Lesson 4 turns the idea of 'equal ratios' into a single number, the linear scale factor k. This gives the precise answer to what must stay the same: one k for all lengths. It prepares learners for similar triangles in Lesson 5, area and volume factors in Lessons 6 and 7, and enlargements with a centre in Lessons 8 to 10.",
      "safetyNotes": "Learners use rulers and scissors with care and walk, not run, when moving to the Driving Question Board (DQB). Keep bags and desks clear. No electricity or internet is needed."
    },
    "overview": "Mrs Achieng revisits Wanjiru's photo from Lakeview Photo Studio, Kisumu. Learners first predict how many times smaller the 3.5 × 4.5 cm passport photo is than the 14 × 18 cm original, and how many times bigger the 70 × 90 cm prize-giving poster is. In groups they then measure and calculate k = image ÷ object for width and for height on each print. They discover that the passport print gives 1/4 for both width and height, the poster gives 5 for both, and passport to poster gives 20. For the distorted 4.5 × 4.5 cm print, the factors are 9/28 for width and 1/4 for height, which are not equal. In the Explain phase learners use k to find missing lengths such as the face-triangle eye gap and chin distance on each print. They then add to the DQB and revise their class model: what must stay the same is one scale factor k applied to every length. The lesson stays on linear lengths only. Angle properties belong to Lesson 3 and area and volume factors to Lessons 6 and 7.",
