        "teacherMoves": "Pose: 'Mr Otieno knows the original eye gap is 5.6 cm. How can he find the eye gap on the passport photo without measuring it?' Give WAIT TIME of 10-15 seconds, then collect 3 responses. Write the rule: 'missing image length = k × object length'. Then ask: 'If you know the image length, how do you find the object length?' Cold-call 3 learners. Ask the groups to write the sentence frame 'k = ___ ÷ ___ because ___'. Challenge: 'The poster is k = 5 from the original. The passport is k = 1/4. Why is passport to poster 20 and not 5?' Allow 15 seconds of silence. Use the answer to restate that the object changes, so k changes. Ask one group to explain why the distorted print is not similar and state that the width and height factors are different. Do not re-teach angle measurement. Refer only to Lesson 3's finding that similar figures have equal ratios of corresponding sides.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Claim-Evidence-Reasoning (CER) with Sentence Frames: learners state a claim (the passport print is similar to the original), support it with calculated evidence (k = 1/4 for both width and height), and reason from the rule (one k for all lengths means the shape is kept). This links calculation to the driving question and supports the NGSS practice of Constructing Explanations.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Listen for: (1) correct missing lengths of 1.4, 1.8, 28 and 36 cm. (2) division by k used to find object lengths. (3) the explanation of why the distorted print has no single k (9/28 ≠ 1/4). Check that at least 3 of the 4 groups state which print is the object. Mark with a tick when a learner uses the words 'corresponding sides'. Spot learners confusing k = 5 with k = 20 and prompt them to name the object and image."
