    "teacherReflection": "After the lesson, reflect: (1) Did learners correctly place negative-k images on the opposite side of O, and how many needed the ray extended backwards? (2) Did they understand that 'enlargement' with 0 < k < 1 produces a reduction, and could they connect it to the 1/4 passport print? (3) Which prediction misconceptions (e.g. 'negative means smaller' or 'inverted means distorted') were most common, and what evidence changed learners' minds? (4) Was WAIT TIME of 10-15 seconds used consistently, and did cold-calling reach quieter learners? (5) Were object and image used consistently in k = image ÷ object? (6) What adjustments to grid paper, torch or sunlight availability would help next time? Use these notes to prepare Lesson 10, where learners find the centre and scale factor from a given object and image.",
