      "learned": "We learned that a scale factor between 0 and 1 gives a reduction, a negative scale factor gives an inverted image on the opposite side of the centre, and lengths are multiplied by the size of k. In every case the corresponding angles stay equal (for example 90°) and the ratios of corresponding sides stay equal, so the image is similar to the object.",
      "explained": "Wanjiru's passport print (3.5 × 4.5 cm) is a reduction of her 14 × 18 cm original with k = 1/4, since both width and height are multiplied by the same factor and the width : height ratio stays 7:9, so her face looks like her. The 4.5 × 4.5 cm print used different factors (9/28 for width and 1/4 for height), so the shape changed and her face looked stretched. Size can change by any scale factor, even a fraction or a negative one, but shape is kept only if one scale factor is applied to all lengths."
