      "skills": "Developing and using models (revising a cumulative model); Constructing explanations (claim-evidence-reasoning); Analysing and interpreting data (comparing ratios, angles and scale factors); Engaging in argument from evidence; Communicating (using the terms corresponding sides, corresponding angles, scale factor k, ~). Measuring with a ruler and protractor.",
      "attitudes": "Appreciate the use of similarity and enlargement in day-to-day activities. Communication and Collaboration: share and defend ideas in groups. Critical Thinking and Problem Solving: use ratios, angles and scale factors to justify a conclusion. Values: Unity (working together to build one group explanation); Responsibility (taking care of rulers, protractors and shared materials). PCI, Life Skills (self-esteem): confidently presenting an explanation to peers.",
      "keyInquiry": "1. Why do we need to enlarge or reduce objects? 2. How do we use similarity and enlargement in day-to-day activities?",
      "purposeInStoryline": "This is the final lesson. In Lesson 1 learners met the puzzle: the size of Wanjiru's photo changes hugely, yet her shape is kept, and they recorded initial ideas about what must stay the same and what may change. Lessons 2 to 11 supplied the evidence: sorting similar figures (L2), equal angles and equal ratios (L3), scale factor k (L4), similar triangles with shadows (L5), area factor k² (L6), volume factor k³ (L7), enlargement from a centre (L8 to L10) and applications to maps and signs (L11). Today learners synthesise this into one Final Explanation, compare their final model with their Lesson 1 model, and resolve every question on the Driving Question Board.",
      "safetyNotes": "Scissors and protractors have sharp points: carry them point down and keep them on desks when not in use. Keep walkways clear when groups display posters. No electricity or internet is needed."
